lens_summaries.review_summary:
I found six real defects on my lens, plus one minor robustness gap, and none is critical. Three are major: (1) local disk on /tmp fills up within one boot because every journal entry is kept locally, and the disk budget leaves this out; (2) a valid `found --raw` result saved on another file system ends in a sticky STOP that needs the maintainer; (3) the MK-1 change to make_kit.py stops a legitimate kit from rebuilding through the parts route. Four are minor: an injected-line leak through answer keys; session_start.sh running without the helper lock; session_start.sh layout checks that differ from the helper's; and non-OSError exceptions that end in a traceback with no block.

The amendment's own mechanisms held up wherever I probed them:
- the A4 leftover sweeps (I found no case where they remove a live file or evidence; they run under the helper lock, and the runner's jobs roots `qual/<root>` and `jobs/<utc14>` match the `*/.validate_*` glob);
- A5 SETUP_MISSING;
- A6, the BASE/tmp emptying and TMPDIR (runner `tool_env` keeps os.environ; `clean_env` passes TMPDIR; visual_qa's soffice HOME is the temp folder);
- the EROFS to READ_ONLY_FS mapping;
- the commit download-back check (partial download gives a slip, a different sha gives COPY_MISMATCH or FORK);
- `durable.write` (temp, fsync, replace, read-back), `tree_list` (links, special files, set-id modes, non-UTF-8 names) and the Archive or kit member checks (type, unsafe path, duplicate, mode).

One finding sits outside the amendment: MK-1 in make_kit.py was added in 746ea98. I also checked the `ensure_local` 'local' downloads, which check size only although md5 is known. I dropped that as a finding: in D1 worlds reset at head 6 (and reset at head 5 or 7 by intent; a bug in my hook meant those resets never happened), no journal file came through that path after the restore. The head-9 run was stopped before it finished. The runner is unchanged (sha256 7505e98a…f431 checked).

Operator-executability review of cps-vm-helper.v2.1 at bbe768f (deploy/vm2 git tree 4f996ef9; the test record shows helper tree sha 55a09073...cf7e and runner sha 7505e98a, which match the claims). I played the operator against the real printed blocks, using the harness's World, FakeDrive and FakeDot with the real runner v4 on invented data. Runs: a Q1 qualification series (small, large with scope reader, nonattempt, and a REVIEW_INCOMPLETE continuation) went through to STOP FINAL HANDBACK B. I also ran an E1 everyday job with its personal copy and delivery, and made targeted mistakes at the raw-save, answer, usage and setup points. A uid-1000 drill with HOME and TOOLS read-only (standing in for the standard route) reached FINAL with no write refused, so the helper itself writes nothing under HOME.

The README's section 7 counts match the harness. One normal run with a continuation and 13 PNG parts took 22 helper commands and 60 Drive calls, which is 16+6 and 15+3p+6. A nonattempt took 2 commands and 3 Drive calls.

Real defects found:
- **Usage errors (exit 2) reprint the block with its UPLOAD lines and no reason.** This contradicts SD-60 and README section 2. FakeDot hides it because it dedups uploads.
- **Disk grows without limit within a boot.** Every confirmed entry archive is kept in inbox/journal until the boot changes. Neither the disk budget nor any test covers it. Around ten large runs in one boot can end in a sticky DISK_FULL.
- **The mailbox protocol and README rule 1/6 contradict each other.** They disagree on who runs maintainer messages and which conversation CLAIM.md names. A HANDBACK C clear has no defined way to reach the Dot while the mailbox check is paused. session_start.sh takes no helper lock.
- **A series needs one unbroken coordinating turn of thousands of tool calls, and some waits never end.** The README gives no safe pause point and no limit on waiting for a G, S or V conversation. Any turn end needs the owner to type "continue".
- **Several minor operator traps.**
  - After a reset, a failed setup is a sticky SETUP_FAILED, not the self-clearable SETUP_MISSING the README describes.
  - Pattern names such as YES have no legend, and four slips on one block are a sticky SLIP_LIMIT.
  - A wrong saved search is silently read as "absent" and triggers a re-upload.
  - README section 7 understates p, the polls and the wall time.
  - Step 0 has no rule for picking among several VM2_RELEASE.md copies.
  - Refused-upload notes are never retired once the helper confirms the file.
  - Everyday JOB.json requirements and the INPUTS_CHANGED recovery depend on retyping, and the personal-copy place conflicts with DEPLOYMENT_MODEL.

Frank judgement: the block discipline itself is well built and the counts are honest. An LLM operator can follow it for one run. Running 72 qualification runs (about 3,600 or more tool calls and several days) is not shown to be executable on this surface. It depends on unproven multi-hour turns, the owner's "continue" after every turn end, and an unresolved mailbox and clear channel. Within one boot it will run into the inbox/journal disk growth.

Only the reset drill (G-4) can show the following:
- that Create Thread conversations read and write /tmp/cps;
- that the Drive upload tool reads /tmp/cps/outbox, including 90 MiB parts, and whether an upload staging copy is needed (M012 mentions an "upload copy");
- where the download tool's attachments land;
- the real search-object format (the created_at format against DRIVE_TIME, paging) and whether it returns trashed or other-folder copies;
- CPSV-BUSY against a standard-route lock holder;
- that 600 to 1,200 s foreground stages survive through the standard route, and the tool's return interval;
- whether a coordinating turn can last hours with hundreds of calls, and whether notes survive context compaction;
- what the mailbox check does during a series and at 6 hours;
- whether /tmp/cps and the boot id survive a reset;
- MemAvailable and /tmp free space over many real-size runs in one boot (inbox/journal growth);
- the bootstrap's elevated review and owner waits after each reset;
- the real finish-stage time against the 1,200 s hold;
- the refusal rate under the G-10 pre-approval.
